1
00:00:08,368 --> 00:00:10,693
What's the emergency? It's mauve.

2
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:13,082
Mauve?

3
00:00:13,122 --> 00:00:17,117
Universally recognised colour
for danger. What happened to red?

4
00:00:17,157 --> 00:00:20,436
That's just humans. By everyone
else's standards, red's camp.

5
00:00:20,476 --> 00:00:24,750
Oh, the misunderstandings! All
those red alerts, all that dancing!

6
00:00:24,790 --> 00:00:30,199
I've hacked into the computer,
slaved the TARDIS. Wherever it goes,
we go! And that's safe? Totally.

7
00:00:33,379 --> 00:00:36,256
OK - reasonably.
Should have said "reasonably" there.

8
00:00:36,296 --> 00:00:41,969
No! It's jumping time tracks,
getting away from us!
What exactly is this thing?

9
00:00:42,009 --> 00:00:48,272
No idea. Then why are we chasing it?
It's mauve and dangerous, and 30
seconds from the centre of London.

10
00:01:35,793 --> 00:01:40,837
D'you know how long you can
knock around space without
happening to bump into Earth?

11
00:01:40,877 --> 00:01:43,668
Five days? Or is that just
when we're out of milk?

12
00:01:43,708 --> 00:01:47,877
All the species in all the universe,
and it has to come out of a cow!

13
00:01:48,857 --> 00:01:52,215
Must've come down
somewhere quite close,

14
00:01:52,255 --> 00:01:56,370
within a mile. And it can't have
been more than a month ago.

15
00:01:56,410 --> 00:01:58,856
A month? We were right behind it.

16
00:01:58,896 --> 00:02:03,723
It was jumping time tracks.
We're bound to be a little bit out.
Do YOU wanna drive?

17
00:02:03,763 --> 00:02:06,160
Yeah. How much is a little?

18
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:10,005
A bit. Is that exactly a bit? Ish.

19
00:02:10,045 --> 00:02:14,072
What's the plan, then?
You gonna do a scan for alien tech?

20
00:02:14,112 --> 00:02:18,668
Rose, it hit the middle of London
with a loud bang. I'm gonna ask.

21
00:02:18,708 --> 00:02:21,462
"Dr John Smith,
Ministry of Asteroids."

22
00:02:21,502 --> 00:02:25,300
It's psychic paper, it tells you. . .
whatever you want it to tell me.

23
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:28,497
Not very Spock, is it, just asking?

24
00:02:28,537 --> 00:02:33,657
Door, music, people - what do you
think? I think you should do
a scan for alien tech.

25
00:02:33,697 --> 00:02:36,488
Gimme some Spock for once!
Would it kill ya?

26
00:02:36,528 --> 00:02:39,405
You sure about that T-shirt?

27
00:02:39,445 --> 00:02:43,402
Too early to say.
I'm taking it out for a spin.

28
00:02:43,442 --> 00:02:46,963
Mummy!

29
00:02:47,916 --> 00:02:49,929
Mummy!

30
00:02:52,719 --> 00:02:55,870
Come on, if you're coming!
It won't take a minute.

31
00:02:57,431 --> 00:03:00,093
Mum-my!

32
00:03:03,784 --> 00:03:07,379
Doctor?
Doctor, there's a kid up there!

33
00:03:31,989 --> 00:03:35,354
# For nobody else

34
00:03:35,394 --> 00:03:38,423
# Gave me the thrill

35
00:03:38,463 --> 00:03:42,576
# With all your faults
I love you still

36
00:03:42,616 --> 00:03:46,060
# It had to be you

37
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,250
# Wonderful you. . . #

38
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:52,357
Are you all right up there? Mum-my!

39
00:03:57,762 --> 00:04:02,267
# . .It had to be you. #

40
00:04:09,871 --> 00:04:13,264
Excuse me! Excuse me, could
I have everybody's attention?

41
00:04:13,304 --> 00:04:17,178
Just for a mo. Be very quick. Hello!
Um. . .

42
00:04:17,218 --> 00:04:22,912
might seem like a stupid question,
but has anything fallen
from the sky recently?

43
00:04:42,394 --> 00:04:44,851
Mummy!

44
00:04:46,352 --> 00:04:49,185
OK, hang on, don't move!

45
00:05:00,302 --> 00:05:03,935
Sorry, have I said something funny?

46
00:05:03,975 --> 00:05:08,965
It's just there's this thing
I need to find. Would've fallen
from the sky a couple of days ago.

47
00:05:11,522 --> 00:05:13,450
Would've landed near here. . .

48
00:05:16,756 --> 00:05:21,261
.with a very loud. . . Quickly
as you can, down to the shelters.

49
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:25,604
.bang.

50
00:05:27,821 --> 00:05:30,449
Mummy?

51
00:05:31,738 --> 00:05:33,421
Balloon!

52
00:05:45,763 --> 00:05:48,675
Doctor!

53
00:05:51,724 --> 00:05:53,918
Doctor!

54
00:05:53,958 --> 00:05:56,136
Doctor!

55
00:06:05,663 --> 00:06:08,291
OK, maybe not this T-shirt.

56
00:06:14,731 --> 00:06:16,255
Rose?

57
00:06:23,084 --> 00:06:26,286
You know, one day. . .

58
00:06:27,799 --> 00:06:29,916
.just one day, maybe,

59
00:06:29,956 --> 00:06:33,832
I'm gonna meet somebody who gets
the whole "Don't wander off" thing.

60
00:06:33,872 --> 00:06:37,871
900 years of phone-box travel, it's
the only thing left to surprise me!

61
00:06:51,978 --> 00:06:54,609
How can you be ringing?

62
00:06:54,649 --> 00:06:59,286
What's that about - ringing?
What am I supposed to do
with a ringing phone?

63
00:06:59,326 --> 00:07:01,322
Don't answer it.

64
00:07:01,362 --> 00:07:03,825
It's not for you.

65
00:07:07,277 --> 00:07:10,195
And how do you know that?
Cos I do.

66
00:07:10,235 --> 00:07:12,392
And I'm telling ya.

67
00:07:12,432 --> 00:07:14,514
Don't answer it.

68
00:07:14,554 --> 00:07:18,422
If you know so much, tell me this -
how can it be ringing?

69
00:07:18,462 --> 00:07:21,664
It's not even a real phone,
it's not connected, it's not. . .

70
00:07:33,853 --> 00:07:35,377
Hello?

71
00:07:37,924 --> 00:07:40,381
This is the Doctor speaking.

72
00:07:41,402 --> 00:07:43,278
How may I help you?

73
00:07:43,318 --> 00:07:45,437
'Mummy?

74
00:07:45,477 --> 00:07:47,396
'Mummy!'

75
00:07:47,436 --> 00:07:49,032
Who is this?

76
00:07:49,072 --> 00:07:50,950
Who's speaking?

77
00:07:50,990 --> 00:07:53,907
'Are you my mummy?' Who is this?

78
00:07:53,947 --> 00:07:56,667
'Mummy!'

79
00:07:56,707 --> 00:08:00,657
How did you ring here? This isn't a
real phone, it's not wired up to. . .

80
00:08:00,697 --> 00:08:04,388
'Mummy?'

81
00:08:09,374 --> 00:08:10,969
Rose?

82
00:08:11,009 --> 00:08:13,325
Rose, are you in there?

83
00:08:24,277 --> 00:08:27,277
The planes are coming.
Can't you hear 'em?

84
00:08:27,317 --> 00:08:31,024
Into the shelter.
None of your nonsense. Now move it!

85
00:08:31,064 --> 00:08:35,370
Come on! Hurry up,
get in there. Come on.

86
00:08:36,263 --> 00:08:40,775
Arthur? Arthur, will you 'urry up? !
Didn't you hear the siren?

87
00:08:40,815 --> 00:08:45,171
Middle of dinner, every night.
Bloomin' Germans! Don't they eat?

88
00:08:45,211 --> 00:08:49,927
I can hear the planes. Don't you eat?
Oh, keep your voice down,
it's an air raid!

89
00:08:53,004 --> 00:08:58,038
Get in! Look, there's a war on.
I know there is! Don't push me.

90
00:09:50,983 --> 00:09:54,499
Get those lights out.
Everyone down to the shelter.

91
00:09:54,539 --> 00:09:56,978
Jack, you going down to the shelter?

92
00:09:57,018 --> 00:10:00,729
Only I've got to go off
on some damn silly guard duty.

93
00:10:00,769 --> 00:10:06,168
Barrage balloon, eh? Must've come
loose. Happens now and then.

94
00:10:06,208 --> 00:10:09,729
Don't you RAF boys
use them for target practice?

95
00:10:11,401 --> 00:10:14,558
Excellent bottom!

96
00:10:14,598 --> 00:10:17,953
I say, old man,
there's a time and a place.

97
00:10:17,993 --> 00:10:20,740
Look, um, should really be off.

98
00:10:22,707 --> 00:10:26,544
Sorry, old man!
I gotta go meet a girl.

99
00:10:26,584 --> 00:10:29,581
But you've got
an excellent bottom too!

100
00:11:20,487 --> 00:11:22,124
Many kids out there?

101
00:11:22,164 --> 00:11:26,122
Yes, Miss. . .Ah! Still carving.

102
00:11:26,162 --> 00:11:27,642
Sit and wait.

103
00:11:27,682 --> 00:11:30,238
Got the whole air raid!

104
00:11:30,278 --> 00:11:34,208
Look at that. Bet it's off
the black market. That's enough!

105
00:11:46,857 --> 00:11:48,779
Wa-a-ah!

106
00:11:53,212 --> 00:11:55,488
OK, I've got you.

107
00:11:55,528 --> 00:11:57,006
Who's got me?

108
00:11:57,046 --> 00:11:59,805
Who's got me and, you know, how?

109
00:11:59,845 --> 00:12:02,164
I'm programming your descent pattern.

110
00:12:02,204 --> 00:12:05,877
Stay still and keep your hands
and feet inside the light field.

111
00:12:05,917 --> 00:12:09,608
Descent pattern? Oh, and can
you switch off your cell phone?

112
00:12:10,597 --> 00:12:14,068
No, seriously. It interferes
with my instruments.

113
00:12:14,108 --> 00:12:16,980
You know no-one ever believes that?

114
00:12:17,945 --> 00:12:19,982
Thank you, that's much better.

115
00:12:20,022 --> 00:12:24,616
Oh, yeah, that's a real load off(!)
I'm hanging in the sky
in the middle of a German air raid,

116
00:12:24,656 --> 00:12:28,613
with a Union Jack across my chest
but, hey, my mobile phone's off(!)

117
00:12:28,653 --> 00:12:31,410
Be with you in a moment.

118
00:12:31,450 --> 00:12:35,647
The mobile communication
device indicates non-contemporaneous
life form.

119
00:12:35,687 --> 00:12:37,325
She's not from around here, no.

120
00:12:37,365 --> 00:12:41,282
Ready for you. Hold tight! To what?

121
00:12:41,322 --> 00:12:43,358
Fair point.

122
00:12:51,349 --> 00:12:54,903
I've got you. You're fine,
you're just fine.

123
00:12:54,943 --> 00:12:58,964
The tractor beam, it can
scramble your head just a little.

124
00:13:00,181 --> 00:13:01,819
Hello.

125
00:13:01,859 --> 00:13:03,934
Hello.

126
00:13:03,974 --> 00:13:06,612
Hello.

127
00:13:06,652 --> 00:13:09,928
Sorry.
There was "hello" twice there.

128
00:13:09,968 --> 00:13:12,166
Dull but, you know, thorough.

129
00:13:12,206 --> 00:13:14,714
Are you all right? I'm fine.

130
00:13:16,763 --> 00:13:19,999
Why, you expecting me to faint?
You look a little dizzy.

131
00:13:20,039 --> 00:13:23,834
What about you?
You're not even in focus. . . !

132
00:13:23,874 --> 00:13:26,257
Oh, boll. . . !

133
00:13:51,088 --> 00:13:55,800
It's got to be black market. You
couldn't get all this on coupons.

134
00:13:55,840 --> 00:14:00,675
Ernie, how many times? We're guests
in this house! We will not make
comments of that kind.

135
00:14:00,715 --> 00:14:03,748
Washing up! Oh, Nancy!

136
00:14:03,788 --> 00:14:06,907
Haven't seen you at one of these
before. He told me about it.

137
00:14:06,947 --> 00:14:09,344
Sleeping rough? Yes, Miss.

138
00:14:09,384 --> 00:14:12,063
All right, then.

139
00:14:12,103 --> 00:14:16,217
One slice each. And I want
to see everybody chewing properly.

140
00:14:16,257 --> 00:14:18,390
- Thank you, Miss.
- Thanks, Miss.

141
00:14:20,135 --> 00:14:23,251
Thank you, Miss!

142
00:14:23,291 --> 00:14:25,608
Thanks, Miss!

143
00:14:25,648 --> 00:14:29,246
It's all right,
everybody stay where they are!

144
00:14:29,286 --> 00:14:32,720
Good here, innit? Who's got
the salt? Back in your seats.

145
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:35,223
He shouldn't be here either.

146
00:14:37,436 --> 00:14:39,631
So, you lot,

147
00:14:39,671 --> 00:14:41,871
what's the story? What do you mean?

148
00:14:41,911 --> 00:14:45,308
You're homeless, right?
Living rough? Why do you wanna know?

149
00:14:45,348 --> 00:14:48,145
You a copper?
Course I'm not a copper.

150
00:14:48,185 --> 00:14:52,935
What's a copper gonna do with you
lot anyway? Arrest you for starving?

151
00:14:53,898 --> 00:14:58,772
I make it 1941. You lot shouldn't
be in London. Should've been
evacuated to the country by now.

152
00:14:58,812 --> 00:15:01,210
I was evacuated. Sent me to a farm.

153
00:15:01,250 --> 00:15:03,527
So why'd you come back?
There was a man there. . . Yeah.

154
00:15:03,567 --> 00:15:05,448
Same with Ernie. Two homes ago.

155
00:15:05,488 --> 00:15:09,042
Shut up! It's better on the
streets anyway. It's better food.

156
00:15:09,082 --> 00:15:13,037
Nancy always gets the best food
for us. So that's what you do, Nancy?

157
00:15:13,077 --> 00:15:16,913
What is? Soon as the sirens go,
you find a big family meal,

158
00:15:16,953 --> 00:15:19,549
everyone's down
in the air raid shelter,

159
00:15:19,589 --> 00:15:25,422
and - bingo - feeding frenzy
for the homeless kids of London!
As long as the bombs don't get you.

160
00:15:25,462 --> 00:15:28,261
Something wrong with that?
Wrong? It's brilliant.

161
00:15:28,301 --> 00:15:34,013
I'm not sure if it's Marxism
in action or a West End musical.
Why did you follow me?

162
00:15:34,053 --> 00:15:38,492
What d'you want?
I want to know how a phone that
isn't a phone gets a phone call.

163
00:15:38,532 --> 00:15:43,846
You seem to be the one to ask.
I did you a favour. I told you not
to answer it. That's all I'm sayin'.

164
00:15:43,886 --> 00:15:47,282
Great, thanks! And I wanna
find a blonde in a Union Jack.

165
00:15:47,322 --> 00:15:54,314
I mean a specific one, I didn't just
wake up this morning with a craving.
Anybody seen a girl like that?

166
00:15:54,354 --> 00:15:56,831
What've I done wrong?
You took two slices.

167
00:15:56,871 --> 00:16:02,264
No blondes, no flags.
Anything else before you leave?
Yeah, there is, actually,

168
00:16:02,304 --> 00:16:07,539
thanks for asking. Something
I'm looking for. Would've fallen
from the sky, but not a bomb.

169
00:16:07,579 --> 00:16:12,296
Not the usual kind, anyway.
Wouldn't have exploded.
Probably just buried itself.

170
00:16:12,336 --> 00:16:16,596
And it would've looked
something like. . .this.

171
00:16:19,529 --> 00:16:23,795
Mummy? Are you in there, Mummy?

172
00:16:25,725 --> 00:16:27,918
Mummy?

173
00:16:27,958 --> 00:16:33,512
Who was the last in? Him. Nah, he
came round the back. Who came in the
front? Me. Did you close the door?

174
00:16:33,552 --> 00:16:36,470
I don't. . . Did you close the door?

175
00:16:36,510 --> 00:16:38,906
Mummy? Mummy?

176
00:16:38,946 --> 00:16:42,103
Mum-my!

177
00:16:47,976 --> 00:16:53,451
What's this? Never easy being the
only child left out in the cold.
I suppose you'd know!

178
00:16:53,491 --> 00:16:55,848
I do actually, yes.

179
00:16:55,888 --> 00:16:57,885
It's not exactly a child.

180
00:16:57,925 --> 00:16:59,568
Mum-my!

181
00:17:01,282 --> 00:17:05,298
Right, everybody out. Across
the back garden. . .Now! Go! Move!

182
00:17:08,831 --> 00:17:11,271
Come on, baby, we've got to go.

183
00:17:11,311 --> 00:17:16,959
It's just like a game, just like
chasing. Take your coat, go on. Go!

184
00:17:18,185 --> 00:17:22,030
Mummy? Mummy?
Please let me in, Mummy.

185
00:17:23,578 --> 00:17:25,776
Please let me in, Mummy.

186
00:17:25,816 --> 00:17:28,688
You all right? Please let me in.

187
00:17:29,333 --> 00:17:32,530
You mustn't let him touch you.
What happens if he touches me?

188
00:17:32,570 --> 00:17:38,218
He'll make you like HIM.
And what's he like? I've gotta go.
Nancy, what's he like.

189
00:17:39,644 --> 00:17:41,721
He's empty.

190
00:17:41,761 --> 00:17:45,156
It's him.

191
00:17:45,196 --> 00:17:47,752
He can make phones ring. He can.

192
00:17:47,792 --> 00:17:50,539
Just like with that police box,
you saw!

193
00:17:55,903 --> 00:17:58,339
'Are you my Mummy?'

194
00:18:01,782 --> 00:18:06,611
'Mummy? Please let me in, Mummy.'

195
00:18:07,532 --> 00:18:11,489
'Mum-my! Mum-my! Mum-my!'

196
00:18:11,529 --> 00:18:14,071
You stay here if you want to!

197
00:18:16,162 --> 00:18:18,279
Mummy. . . ?

198
00:18:18,319 --> 00:18:23,831
Let me in, please, Mummy.
Please let me in!

199
00:18:23,871 --> 00:18:25,389
Your Mummy isn't here.

200
00:18:26,907 --> 00:18:29,908
Are you my Mummy? No mummies here.

201
00:18:29,948 --> 00:18:34,541
Nobody here but us chickens.
.Well, this chicken.

202
00:18:34,581 --> 00:18:36,589
I'm scared.

203
00:18:37,539 --> 00:18:40,416
Why are those other children
frightened of you?

204
00:18:40,456 --> 00:18:44,603
Please let me in, Mummy.
I'm scared of the bombs.

205
00:18:47,290 --> 00:18:48,967
OK.

206
00:18:49,007 --> 00:18:51,219
I'm opening the door now.

207
00:19:20,855 --> 00:19:24,052
Better now? You got lights in here?

208
00:19:27,288 --> 00:19:30,085
Hello. Hello. Hello.

209
00:19:30,125 --> 00:19:32,202
Let's not start that again.

210
00:19:32,242 --> 00:19:33,555
OK.

211
00:19:36,556 --> 00:19:39,709
So, um,
who are you supposed be, then?

212
00:19:39,749 --> 00:19:46,864
Captain Jack Harkness, 133 Squadron,
Royal Air Force, American volunteer.

213
00:19:46,904 --> 00:19:51,499
Liar. This is psychic paper,
it tells me whatever you want it to.

214
00:19:51,539 --> 00:19:57,774
How d'you know? Two things.
One, I have a friend that uses this
all the time, and, two. . .

215
00:19:57,814 --> 00:20:01,490
you handed me a piece of paper
saying you're single and work out.

216
00:20:01,530 --> 00:20:06,763
Tricky thing, psychic paper.
Yeah. Can't let your mind wander
when you're handing it over.

217
00:20:06,803 --> 00:20:10,601
Oh, you "sort of"
have a boyfriend called Mickey Smith

218
00:20:10,641 --> 00:20:13,834
but you consider yourself
to be footloose and fancy free.

219
00:20:13,874 --> 00:20:17,471
What? Actually the word you use
is "available".

220
00:20:17,511 --> 00:20:22,706
No way! And another one - "very".
Shall we try and get along
without the psychic papers?

221
00:20:22,746 --> 00:20:25,243
That would be better, wouldn't it?

222
00:20:26,503 --> 00:20:29,818
Nice spaceship. Gets me around.

223
00:20:29,858 --> 00:20:32,658
Very. . .

224
00:20:32,698 --> 00:20:35,454
Spock. Who?

225
00:20:35,494 --> 00:20:38,491
Guessing you're not
a local boy, then?

226
00:20:38,531 --> 00:20:44,605
A cell phone, a liquid-crystal watch
and fabrics that won't be around
for at least another two decades.

227
00:20:44,645 --> 00:20:48,211
Guessing you're not a local girl.
Guessing right.

228
00:20:50,436 --> 00:20:53,233
Burn your hands on the rope? Yeah.

229
00:20:53,273 --> 00:20:57,587
We're parked in mid-air.
Can't anyone down there see us? No.

230
00:20:57,627 --> 00:21:01,808
Can I have a look at your hands
for a moment? Why? Please?

231
00:21:09,059 --> 00:21:11,095
You can stop acting now.

232
00:21:11,135 --> 00:21:13,412
I know exactly who you are.

233
00:21:13,452 --> 00:21:16,330
I can spot a Time Agent a mile away.

234
00:21:16,370 --> 00:21:20,407
A Time Agent? Mmm, I've been
expecting one of you guys to show up.

235
00:21:20,447 --> 00:21:24,201
Though not, I must say, by barrage
balloon! Often travel that way?

236
00:21:24,241 --> 00:21:28,074
Sometimes I get swept off my feet.

237
00:21:28,114 --> 00:21:30,472
B. . .by balloons!

238
00:21:30,512 --> 00:21:33,635
What are you doing?
Try to keep still.

239
00:21:39,506 --> 00:21:44,711
Nanogenes. Subatomic robots.
The air in here is full of them.

240
00:21:46,817 --> 00:21:50,094
They just repaired three
layers of your skin.

241
00:21:50,134 --> 00:21:52,811
Oh! Well, tell 'em, thanks.

242
00:21:52,851 --> 00:21:56,088
Shall we get down to business?
Business?

243
00:21:56,128 --> 00:21:59,979
Shall we have a drink on the balcony?

244
00:22:01,482 --> 00:22:03,245
Bring up the glasses.

245
00:22:15,064 --> 00:22:17,771
I know I'm standing on something. . .

246
00:22:26,777 --> 00:22:31,369
OK, you have
an invisible space ship. . . Yeah.

247
00:22:31,409 --> 00:22:34,847
.tethered up to Big Ben
for some reason!

248
00:22:34,887 --> 00:22:37,243
First rule of active camouflage.

249
00:22:37,283 --> 00:22:39,320
Park somewhere you'll remember.

250
00:23:28,510 --> 00:23:32,304
How'd you follow me here? I'm good
at following. Got the nose for it!

251
00:23:32,344 --> 00:23:36,219
People can't usually follow me.
My nose has special powers.

252
00:23:36,259 --> 00:23:38,698
Yeah? Is that why it's so. . . What?

253
00:23:38,738 --> 00:23:40,738
Nothin'. What?

254
00:23:40,778 --> 00:23:42,813
Nothin'!

255
00:23:42,853 --> 00:23:47,369
Do your ears have special powers
too? What are you trying to say?
Good night, Mister.

256
00:23:47,409 --> 00:23:51,803
Nancy. . .there's something
chasing you and the other kids.

257
00:23:51,843 --> 00:23:57,118
It looks like a boy
and it isn't a boy. And it started
about a month ago. Right?

258
00:23:57,158 --> 00:24:02,151
The thing I'm looking for.
The thing that fell from the sky.
That's when it landed.

259
00:24:02,191 --> 00:24:05,827
And you know what I'm talking about,
don't you? There was a bomb.

260
00:24:05,867 --> 00:24:10,542
A bomb that wasn't a bomb.
Fell the other end of Limehouse
Green Station. Take me there.

261
00:24:10,582 --> 00:24:13,340
Soldiers guarding it. Barbed wire.

262
00:24:13,380 --> 00:24:16,377
You'd never get through. Try me!

263
00:24:16,417 --> 00:24:20,094
Sure you want to know what's going
on in there? I really wanna know.

264
00:24:20,134 --> 00:24:24,448
Then there's someone
you need to talk to first.
And who might that be?

265
00:24:24,488 --> 00:24:27,030
The doctor.

266
00:24:30,165 --> 00:24:33,079
It's getting late.
I should be getting back.

267
00:24:33,119 --> 00:24:34,837
We're discussing business.

268
00:24:34,877 --> 00:24:37,753
This isn't business,
this is champagne.

269
00:24:37,793 --> 00:24:41,390
I try never to discuss business
with a clear head.

270
00:24:44,306 --> 00:24:48,981
Are you travelling alone? Are you
authorised to negotiate with me?

271
00:24:49,021 --> 00:24:51,858
What would we be negotiating?

272
00:24:51,898 --> 00:24:55,735
I have something for the Time Agency.
Something they'd like to buy.

273
00:24:55,775 --> 00:24:58,172
Are you empowered to make payment?

274
00:24:58,212 --> 00:25:00,649
Well, I, I should talk to my. . .

275
00:25:00,689 --> 00:25:04,646
companion. "Companion"? I should
really be getting back to him.

276
00:25:04,686 --> 00:25:08,377
"Him"? Do you have the time?

277
00:25:13,514 --> 00:25:16,552
OK, that was flash!

278
00:25:16,592 --> 00:25:19,429
That was on the flash side.

279
00:25:19,469 --> 00:25:22,986
So, when you say your "companion",

280
00:25:23,026 --> 00:25:27,500
just how disappointed should I be?
OK. We're standing in mid-air,

281
00:25:27,540 --> 00:25:31,616
on a spaceship,
during a German air raid.

282
00:25:31,656 --> 00:25:36,152
Do you really think now's
a good time to be coming on to me?

283
00:25:36,192 --> 00:25:40,189
Perhaps not.
Well, it was just a suggestion.

284
00:25:40,229 --> 00:25:42,441
Do you like Glenn Miller?

285
00:25:52,614 --> 00:25:55,251
It's 1941,

286
00:25:55,291 --> 00:25:59,165
height of the London blitz, height
of the German bombing campaign.

287
00:25:59,205 --> 00:26:02,721
And something else
has fallen on London -

288
00:26:02,761 --> 00:26:06,159
a fully-equipped Chula warship,

289
00:26:06,199 --> 00:26:10,396
the last one in existence.
Armed to the teeth.

290
00:26:10,436 --> 00:26:12,632
And I know where it is,

291
00:26:12,672 --> 00:26:15,419
because I parked it.

292
00:26:16,431 --> 00:26:19,667
If the agency can name
the right price,

293
00:26:19,707 --> 00:26:21,863
I can get it for you.

294
00:26:21,903 --> 00:26:24,657
But in two hours,

295
00:26:24,697 --> 00:26:29,353
a German bomb is going to fall on it
and destroy it forever.

296
00:26:29,393 --> 00:26:32,631
That's your deadline.
That's the deal.

297
00:26:32,671 --> 00:26:34,708
Now, shall we discuss payment?

298
00:26:34,748 --> 00:26:37,348
You know what I think? What?

299
00:26:37,388 --> 00:26:39,505
I think you were talking just there.

300
00:26:39,545 --> 00:26:44,059
Two hours, the bomb falls.
There'll be nothing left
but dust and a crater.

301
00:26:44,099 --> 00:26:45,776
Promises, promises!

302
00:26:45,816 --> 00:26:47,817
Are you listening to any of this?

303
00:26:47,857 --> 00:26:51,930
You used to be a Time Agent,
now you're some kind of freelancer.

304
00:26:51,970 --> 00:26:55,090
A little harsh! I like to think
of myself as a criminal.

305
00:26:55,130 --> 00:26:57,806
I'll bet you do.

306
00:26:57,846 --> 00:27:01,121
So this companion of yours,
does he handle the business?

307
00:27:01,161 --> 00:27:03,878
Well, I delegate
a lot of that, yeah.

308
00:27:03,918 --> 00:27:07,991
Well, maybe we should go find him.
And how you gonna do that?

309
00:27:08,031 --> 00:27:11,597
Easy - I'll do a scan for alien tech.

310
00:27:13,787 --> 00:27:16,699
Finally - a professional!

311
00:27:26,214 --> 00:27:31,171
The bomb's under that tarpaulin.
They put the fence up overnight.

312
00:27:31,211 --> 00:27:34,688
See that building? The hospital?

313
00:27:34,728 --> 00:27:37,162
What about it?

314
00:27:37,202 --> 00:27:39,130
That's where the doctor is.

315
00:27:42,516 --> 00:27:46,590
You should talk to him.
For now, I'm more interested
in getting in THERE.

316
00:27:46,630 --> 00:27:49,631
Talk to the doctor first. Why?

317
00:27:49,671 --> 00:27:53,317
Because then maybe
you won't wanna get inside.

318
00:27:55,381 --> 00:27:57,394
Where you going?

319
00:27:58,581 --> 00:28:03,773
There was a lot of food
in that house. I've got mouths
to feed. Should be safe enough now.

320
00:28:03,813 --> 00:28:07,172
Can I ask you a question?

321
00:28:07,212 --> 00:28:10,573
Who did you lose? What?

322
00:28:12,927 --> 00:28:17,557
The way you look after all those
kids. It's cos you lost somebody,
isn't it?

323
00:28:17,597 --> 00:28:20,185
You're doing all this
to make up for it.

324
00:28:22,872 --> 00:28:24,870
My little brother.

325
00:28:24,910 --> 00:28:26,830
Jamie.

326
00:28:26,870 --> 00:28:31,463
One night I went out looking for
food. Same night that thing fell.

327
00:28:31,503 --> 00:28:34,703
Told him not to follow me.
I told him it was dangerous.

328
00:28:34,743 --> 00:28:37,300
But he just. . .

329
00:28:37,340 --> 00:28:40,577
he just didn't like
being on his own. What happened?

330
00:28:40,617 --> 00:28:45,116
In the middle of an air raid?
What d'you think happened?

331
00:28:49,485 --> 00:28:52,002
Amazing! What is?

332
00:28:52,042 --> 00:28:56,515
1941. Right now,
not very far from here,

333
00:28:56,555 --> 00:29:00,474
the German war machine
is rolling up the map of Europe.

334
00:29:00,514 --> 00:29:05,667
Country after country,
falling like dominoes.
Nothing can stop it, nothing.

335
00:29:05,707 --> 00:29:08,145
Until one tiny, damp, little island

336
00:29:08,185 --> 00:29:12,980
says, "No! No. . .not here."

337
00:29:14,859 --> 00:29:18,010
A mouse in front of a lion.

338
00:29:19,495 --> 00:29:22,013
You're amazing, the lot of you.

339
00:29:22,053 --> 00:29:25,619
Dunno what you do to Hitler.
You frighten the hell out of me!

340
00:29:27,083 --> 00:29:30,520
Off you go, then.
Do what you've got to do.

341
00:29:30,560 --> 00:29:32,158
Save the world.

342
00:31:19,962 --> 00:31:22,239
You'll find them everywhere.

343
00:31:22,279 --> 00:31:25,875
In every bed, in every ward.
Hundreds of them.

344
00:31:25,915 --> 00:31:27,435
Yes, I saw.

345
00:31:27,475 --> 00:31:30,790
Why are they still wearing
gas masks? They're not. Who are you?

346
00:31:30,830 --> 00:31:32,950
I'm. . . Are you the doctor?

347
00:31:32,990 --> 00:31:36,147
Dr Constantine. And you are?

348
00:31:36,187 --> 00:31:39,024
Nancy sent me. Nancy?

349
00:31:39,064 --> 00:31:44,019
That means you must have been
asking about the bomb. Yes.

350
00:31:44,059 --> 00:31:48,855
What do you know about it? Nothing.
Why I was asking. What do you know?

351
00:31:48,895 --> 00:31:53,888
Only what it's done.
These people, they were all
caught up in the blast?

352
00:31:53,928 --> 00:31:56,003
None of them were.

353
00:32:02,198 --> 00:32:04,115
You're very sick.

354
00:32:04,155 --> 00:32:09,190
Dying, I should think.
I just haven't been able
to find the time! Are you a doctor?

355
00:32:09,230 --> 00:32:12,228
I have my moments.
Have you examined any of them yet?

356
00:32:12,268 --> 00:32:15,903
No. Don't touch the flesh.

357
00:32:15,943 --> 00:32:18,451
Which one? ANY one.

358
00:32:24,537 --> 00:32:28,651
Conclusions? Massive head trauma,
mostly to the left side.

359
00:32:28,691 --> 00:32:31,291
Partial collapse
of the chest cavity to the right.

360
00:32:31,331 --> 00:32:33,085
Scarring on the back of the hand.

361
00:32:33,125 --> 00:32:36,602
The gas mask seems to be fused to
the flesh but I can't see any burns.

362
00:32:36,642 --> 00:32:38,081
Examine another one.

363
00:32:49,830 --> 00:32:52,981
This isn't possible.
And examine another.

364
00:32:55,941 --> 00:33:00,098
This isn't possible! No.
They've all got the same injuries.

365
00:33:00,138 --> 00:33:05,172
Yes. Exactly the same! Yes.
Right down to the scar
on the back of the hand.

366
00:33:07,129 --> 00:33:12,202
How did this happen? How did
it start? When that bomb dropped,
there was just ONE victim. Dead?

367
00:33:12,242 --> 00:33:16,277
At first.
His injuries were truly dreadful.

368
00:33:16,317 --> 00:33:20,954
By the following morning every doctor
and nurse who had treated him,

369
00:33:20,994 --> 00:33:22,991
who had touched him,

370
00:33:23,031 --> 00:33:25,508
had those exact same injuries.

371
00:33:25,548 --> 00:33:30,304
By the morning after that
every patient on the same ward,
the exact same injuries.

372
00:33:30,344 --> 00:33:33,422
Within a week, the entire hospital.

373
00:33:33,462 --> 00:33:38,056
Physical injuries. . .as plague.

374
00:33:38,096 --> 00:33:40,694
Can you explain that?

375
00:33:40,734 --> 00:33:44,891
What would you say
was the cause of death?

376
00:33:44,931 --> 00:33:47,444
The head trauma. No. Asphyxiation?

377
00:33:47,484 --> 00:33:51,562
No. The collapse of the chest cavity.
No. All right. . .

378
00:33:51,602 --> 00:33:54,757
what was the cause of death?

379
00:33:54,797 --> 00:33:57,476
There wasn't one.

380
00:33:57,516 --> 00:33:59,444
They're not dead.

381
00:34:04,710 --> 00:34:08,543
It's all right, they're harmless.

382
00:34:08,583 --> 00:34:12,381
They just sort of sit there.
There's no heartbeat,

383
00:34:12,421 --> 00:34:14,697
no life signs of any kind.

384
00:34:14,737 --> 00:34:16,455
They just. . .

385
00:34:16,495 --> 00:34:18,135
don't die.

386
00:34:18,175 --> 00:34:24,646
They're just left here? Nobody's
DOING anything? I try and make them
comfortable. What else is there?

387
00:34:24,686 --> 00:34:29,839
Just you? You're the only one here?
Before this war began I was
a father and a grandfather.

388
00:34:29,879 --> 00:34:33,517
Now I'm neither.
But I'm still a doctor.

389
00:34:33,557 --> 00:34:38,551
Yeah. Know the feeling. I suspect
the plan is to blow up the hospital

390
00:34:38,591 --> 00:34:40,628
and blame it on a German bomb.

391
00:34:40,668 --> 00:34:43,628
Probably too late. I know.

392
00:34:43,668 --> 00:34:47,702
There are isolated cases. . .

393
00:34:47,742 --> 00:34:51,620
.isolated cases breaking out. . .
all over. . .London.

394
00:34:51,660 --> 00:34:54,043
Stay back! Stay back!

395
00:34:55,016 --> 00:34:57,333
Listen to me!

396
00:34:57,373 --> 00:35:00,925
Top floor, Room 802.

397
00:35:00,965 --> 00:35:04,764
That's where they took the first
victim, the one from the crash site.

398
00:35:04,804 --> 00:35:07,682
And you must find Nancy again. Nancy?

399
00:35:07,722 --> 00:35:11,435
It was her brother.
She knows more than she's saying.

400
00:35:11,475 --> 00:35:14,097
She won't tell me,
but she m. . .

401
00:35:19,150 --> 00:35:21,687
Mum-meee. . .

402
00:35:24,464 --> 00:35:26,940
are... you...

403
00:35:26,980 --> 00:35:31,081
my. . .mummy?

404
00:35:57,628 --> 00:35:59,186
Her brother!

405
00:36:00,144 --> 00:36:03,541
Hello? Hello?

406
00:36:03,581 --> 00:36:05,475
Hello?

407
00:36:08,657 --> 00:36:13,490
Good evening. Hope we're not
interrupting. Jack Harkness.
I've been hearing all about you.

408
00:36:13,530 --> 00:36:17,210
He knows. I had to tell him.
.About us being Time Agents.

409
00:36:18,723 --> 00:36:22,454
And it's a real pleasure
to meet you, Mr Spock.

410
00:36:23,918 --> 00:36:27,474
Mr Spock? What was I supposed
to say? You don't have a name.

411
00:36:27,514 --> 00:36:31,990
Don't you ever get tired
of "Doctor" Dr WHO? !
Nine centuries in, I'm coping.

412
00:36:32,030 --> 00:36:35,507
Where have you been?
It's not a good time for a stroll!

413
00:36:35,547 --> 00:36:38,344
Who's strollin'?
I went by barrage balloon.

414
00:36:38,384 --> 00:36:40,621
Only way to see an air raid. What?

415
00:36:40,661 --> 00:36:42,794
Listen, what's a Chula warship?

416
00:36:43,861 --> 00:36:45,670
Chula?

417
00:36:52,046 --> 00:36:55,406
Please, Mummy. Please let me in.

418
00:36:55,446 --> 00:36:59,297
I'm scared of the bombs, Mummy.

419
00:37:00,359 --> 00:37:03,437
Mummy?

420
00:37:03,477 --> 00:37:05,894
Mummy!

421
00:37:21,218 --> 00:37:24,496
This just isn't possible.
How did this happen?

422
00:37:24,536 --> 00:37:28,767
What kind of Chula ship landed here?
What? He said it was a warship.

423
00:37:28,807 --> 00:37:34,325
He stole it, parked it somewhere,
somewhere a bomb's gonna to fall on
it. Unless we make him an offer.

424
00:37:34,365 --> 00:37:39,955
What kind of warship? Does it
matter? It's got nothing to do with
this. This started at the bomb site.

425
00:37:39,995 --> 00:37:44,632
It's got everything to do with it.
What kind of warship? An ambulance!

426
00:37:44,672 --> 00:37:48,510
Look, this is what chased you
through the time vortex.

427
00:37:48,550 --> 00:37:53,144
It's space junk.
I wanted to kid you it was valuable.

428
00:37:53,184 --> 00:37:56,100
It's empty. I made sure of it.
Nothing but a shell.

429
00:37:56,140 --> 00:37:59,378
I threw it at you,
saw your time-travel vehicle -

430
00:37:59,418 --> 00:38:01,815
love the retro look, by the way -

431
00:38:01,855 --> 00:38:07,449
threw you the bait. . . Bait? I wanted
to sell it to you, then destroy it
before you found out it was junk.

432
00:38:07,489 --> 00:38:10,976
You said it was a warship.
They have ambulances in wars.

433
00:38:13,400 --> 00:38:18,156
It's a con. I was conning you.
That's what I am, I'm a con man!

434
00:38:18,196 --> 00:38:22,791
Thought you were Time Agents.
You're not, are you?
Just a couple more freelancers.

435
00:38:22,831 --> 00:38:27,228
I should've known, the way you guys
are blending with the local colour!

436
00:38:27,268 --> 00:38:30,662
Flag Girl was bad enough,
but U-Boat Captain? !

437
00:38:30,702 --> 00:38:35,298
Anyway, whatever's happening here
has got nothing to do with that ship.

438
00:38:35,338 --> 00:38:38,496
What IS happening here, Doctor?

439
00:38:38,536 --> 00:38:41,133
Human DNA is being rewritten.

440
00:38:41,173 --> 00:38:43,487
By an idiot. What do you mean?

441
00:38:43,527 --> 00:38:48,962
I don't know, some kind of virus
converting human beings
into these things. But why?

442
00:38:49,002 --> 00:38:50,611
What's the point?

443
00:38:57,035 --> 00:39:00,031
Where's my mummy?

444
00:39:27,362 --> 00:39:30,308
Are you my mummy?

445
00:39:41,348 --> 00:39:43,805
What's happening? I don't know.

446
00:39:50,419 --> 00:39:52,536
It's me - Nancy!

447
00:39:52,576 --> 00:39:57,529
Mummy! Mummy!
Don't let 'em touch you!
What happens if they touch us?

448
00:39:57,569 --> 00:40:00,406
You're looking at it!

449
00:40:00,446 --> 00:40:05,036
Are you my mummy?
It's Nancy, your sister.

450
00:40:07,958 --> 00:40:10,715
You're dead, Jamie. You're dead.

451
00:41:06,259 --> 00:41:09,255
Believe me I'd nothing to do with it!

452
00:41:09,295 --> 00:41:14,567
I'll tell you what's happening,
you forgot to set your alarm clock.
It's Volcano Day! Ooooh!

453
00:41:14,607 --> 00:41:19,245
That door should hold it for a bit.
The door? ! The wall didn't stop it!

454
00:41:19,285 --> 00:41:22,321
Halt! Don't move!
Go on ask me anything, I'm on fire!

455
00:41:22,361 --> 00:41:26,994
You've got the moves, show me
your moves. The world doesn't end
cos The Doctor dances.

456
00:41:27,034 --> 00:41:28,674
Stay back!

457
00:41:28,714 --> 00:41:31,948
Mummy! Doctor!

458
00:41:31,988 --> 00:41:34,107
Secure those gates! Just do it!

459
00:41:34,147 --> 00:41:39,102
All that weapons tech
in the hands of a four-year-old
looking for his mummy

460
00:41:39,142 --> 00:41:42,048
and nothing in the world
can stop it!

461
00:41:46,600 --> 00:41:51,127
Subtitles by BBC Broadcast 2005
DVB-SRT Conversion by reirei.
